oor $, then it is the n-simplex \\alpha _n$\\alpha _n$.\n\n### Example 2.5\n\nThe join \\alpha _{n}*\\alpha _{m}$\\alpha _{n}*\\alpha _{m}$ with n\\le m$n\\le m$ is a thin chamber complex of rank n+m$n+m$ which is n-neighborly and not (n+1)$(n+1)$-neighborly.\n\n### Lemma 3.2\n\nIf A is an automorphism of \\Delta $\\Delta $, then for every flag F we have AT(F)=\\textit{TA}(F)$AT(F)=\\textit{TA}(F)$.\n\n### 3.2 Zigzags and Their Shadows\n\nFor every flag F the sequence\n\n\\begin{aligned} Z=\\{T^{i}(F)\\}_{i\\in {\\mathbb N}} \\end{aligned}\n\n$$\\begin{aligned} Z=\\{T^{i}(F)\\}_{i\\in {\\mathbb N}} \\end{aligned}$$\n\n(we assume that 0 belongs to {\\mathbb N}${\\mathbb N}$) is called a zigzag. Since our simplicial complex is finite, we have T^{l}(F)=F$T^{l}(F)=F$ for some l>0$l>0$. The smallest number l>0$l>0$ satisfying this condition is said to be the length of the zigzag. For any number i the sequence\n\n\\begin{aligned} T^{i}(F),T^{i+1}(F),\\ldots \\end{aligned}\n\n$$\\begin{aligned} T^{i}(F),T^{i+1}(F),\\ldots \\end{aligned}$$\n\nis also a zigzag; it is obtained from Z by a cyclic permutation of the flags. All such zigzags will be identified with Z.\n\nThe k-shadow of a zigzag \\{F_{i}\\}_{i\\in {\\mathbb N}}$\\{F_{i}\\}_{i\\in {\\mathbb N}}$ is the sequence \\{X_{i}\\}_{i\\in {\\mathbb N}}$\\{X_{i}\\}_{i\\in {\\mathbb N}}$, where every X_{i}$X_{i}$ is the k-face from the flag F_{i}$F_{i}$.\n\n### Proposition 3.3\n\nEvery zigzag can be uniquely reconstructed from any of the shadows.\n\n### Proof\n\nLet Z=\\{F_{i}\\}_{i\\in {\\mathbb N}}$Z=\\{F_{i}\\}_{i\\in {\\mathbb N}}$ be a zigzag of length l and let \\{X_{i}\\}_{i\\in {\\mathbb N}}$\\{X_{i}\\}_{i\\in {\\mathbb N}}$ be the k-shadow of Z. The required statement is a consequence of the following two observations. If k>0$k>0$ then the (k-1)$(k-1)$-shadow is the sequence\n\n\\begin{aligned} X_{l-1}\\cap X_{0},\\,X_{0}\\cap X_{1},\\,X_{1}\\cap X_{2},\\dots \\end{aligned}\n\n$$\\begin{aligned} X_{l-1}\\cap X_{0},\\,X_{0}\\cap X_{1},\\,X_{1}\\cap X_{2},\\dots \\end{aligned}$$\n\nSimilarly, if k< n-1$k< n-1$ then the (k+1)$(k+1)$-shadow consists of all X_{i}\\cup X_{i+1}$X_{i}\\cup X_{i+1}$. \\square $\\square $\n\n### Proposition 3.4\n\nIf a sequence Z=\\{F_{1},\\ldots , F_{l}\\}$Z=\\{F_{1},\\ldots , F_{l}\\}$ is a zigzag of length l, then the same holds for the sequence\n\n\\begin{aligned} R(Z)=\\big \\{R(F_{l}),R(F_{l-1}),\\ldots ,R(F_{1})\\big \\}. \\end{aligned}\n\n$$\\begin{aligned} R(Z)=\\big \\{R(F_{l}),R(F_{l-1}),\\ldots ,R(F_{1})\\big \\}. \\end{aligned}$$\n\n### Proof\n\nIf Z=\\{F_{1},\\ldots , F_{l}\\}$Z=\\{F_{1},\\ldots , F_{l}\\}$ is a zigzag, then for every i\\in [l]$i\\in [l]$ we have F_{i}=T^{i-1}(F)$F_{i}=T^{i-1}(F)$, where F=F_{1}$F=F_{1}$. By Lemma 3.1,\n\n\\begin{aligned} \\textit{TR}(F_{i})=\\textit{TRT}^{i-1}(F)=RT^{i-2}(F)=R(F_{i-1}) \\end{aligned}\n\n$$\\begin{aligned} \\textit{TR}(F_{i})=\\textit{TRT}^{i-1}(F)=RT^{i-2}(F)=R(F_{i-1}) \\end{aligned}$$\n\nif i\\ge 2$i\\ge 2$ and\n\n\\begin{aligned} TR(F_{1})= \\textit{TRT }^{l}(F)=RT^{l-1}(F)=R(F_{l}). \\end{aligned}\n\n$$\\begin{aligned} TR(F_{1})= \\textit{TRT }^{l}(F)=RT^{l-1}(F)=R(F_{l}). \\end{aligned}$$\n\nThis means that R(Z) is a zigzag of length l. \\square $\\square $\n\nFollowing [7, Def. 8.2], we say that the zigzag R(Z) is the reverse of Z.\n\n\n\\begin{aligned} \\{x_{1}\\}\\subset \\{x_{1},x_{2}\\}\\subset \\cdots \\subset \\{x_{1},\\ldots , x_{n}\\}. \\end{aligned}\n\n$$\\begin{aligned} \\{x_{1}\\}\\subset \\{x_{1},x_{2}\\}\\subset \\cdots \\subset \\{x_{1},\\ldots , x_{n}\\}. \\end{aligned}$$\n\nWe apply the same arguments to the latter flag and get a certain vertex x_{n+1}$x_{n+1}$. Recurrently, we construct a sequence of vertices \\{x_{i}\\}_{i\\in {\\mathbb N}}$\\{x_{i}\\}_{i\\in {\\mathbb N}}$ such that T^{i}(F)$T^{i}(F)$ is the flag\n\n\\begin{aligned} \\{x_{i}\\}\\subset \\{x_{i},x_{i+1}\\}\\subset \\cdots \\subset \\{x_{i},\\ldots ,x_{i+n-1}\\}. \\end{aligned}\n\n$$\\begin{aligned} \\{x_{i}\\}\\subset \\{x_{i},x_{i+1}\\}\\subset \\cdots \\subset \\{x_{i},\\ldots ,x_{i+n-1}\\}. \\end{aligned}$$\n\n(3)\n\nThe sequence \\{x_{i}\\}_{i\\in {\\mathbb N}}$\\{x_{i}\\}_{i\\in {\\mathbb N}}$ is the 0-shadow of the zigzag \\{T^{i}(F)\\}_{i\\in {\\mathbb N}}$\\{T^{i}(F)\\}_{i\\in {\\mathbb N}}$. For every i\\in {\\mathbb N}$i\\in {\\mathbb N}$ the following assertions are fulfilled:\n\n  1. (Z1)\n\nx_{i}, x_{i+1},\\ldots , x_{i+n-1}$x_{i}, x_{i+1},\\ldots , x_{i+n-1}$ form a facet,\n\n  2. (Z2)\n\nx_{i}\\ne x_{n+i}$x_{i}\\ne x_{n+i}$.\n\nIf l is the length of the zigzag, then l>n$l>n$ and x_{i+l}=x_{i}$x_{i+l}=x_{i}$ for all i\\in {\\mathbb N}$i\\in {\\mathbb N}$. The equality x_{i}=x_{j}$x_{i}=x_{j}$ is possible for some distinct i,j\\in \\{0,1,\\ldots ,l-1\\}$i,j\\in \\{0,1,\\ldots ,l-1\\}$ only in the case when |i-j|>n$|i-j|>n$. We say that the zigzag \\{T^{i}(F)\\}_{i\\in {\\mathbb N}}$\\{T^{i}(F)\\}_{i\\in {\\mathbb N}}$ is simple if x_{0},x_{1}.\\ldots ,x_{l-1}$x_{0},x_{1}.\\ldots ,x_{l-1}$ are mutually distinct. In this case, the k-shadow is formed by l mutually distinct k-faces for every k. The complex \\Delta $\\Delta $ is said to be z-simple if every zigzag is simple.\n\n\nWe say that \\Delta $\\Delta $ is z-uniform if all zigzags are of the same length.\n\n### Lemma 3.7\n\nIf \\Delta $\\Delta $ is z-uniform and the length of zigzags is equal to l, then there are precisely n!N / 2l zigzags, where N is the number of facets in \\Delta $\\Delta $.\n\n### Proof\n\nBy the definition, zigzags are the orbits of the action of the operator T on the set of flags. Thus, the sum of the lengths of all zigzags is equal to the number of flags. There are precisely n!N distinct flags. Since every zigzag is identified with its reverse, we get precisely n!N / 2l zigzags. \\square $\\square $\n\n### Example 3.8\n\nThere is a natural one-to-one correspondence between zigzags of \\alpha _{n}$\\alpha _{n}$ and permutation on the set [n+1]$[n+1]$. Every zigzag is of length n+1$n+1$ and Lemma 3.7 implies that the number of zigzag is equal to \\frac{n!}{2}$\\frac{n!}{2}$.\n\nThere is the following characterization of n-simplex in terms of the length of zigzags.\n\n### Proposition 3.9\n\nSuppose that \\Delta $\\Delta $ is a thin chamber complex. Then it contains a zigzag of length n+1$n+1$ if and only if it is the n-simplex \\alpha _{n}$\\alpha _{n}$.\n\n### Proof\n\nBy Example 3.8, every zigzag in \\alpha _{n}$\\alpha _{n}$ is of length n+1$n+1$. Conversely, suppose that Z is a zigzag of length n+1$n+1$. It follows from (Z1) and (Z2) that the 0-shadow of Z consists of n+1$n+1$ mutually distinct vertices. Denote by X the subset of V formed by these n+1$n+1$ vertices. By (Z1), every n-element subset of X is a facet of \\Delta $\\Delta $. Since every (n-1)$(n-1)$-element subset of X is the intersection of two such facets, the connectedness of \\Gamma _{n-1}(\\Delta )$\\Gamma _{n-1}(\\Delta )$ guarantees that there are no other facets in \\Delta $\\Delta $. \\square $\\square $\n\n### Example 3.10\n\nThe 0-shadow of every zigzag in \\beta _{n}$\\beta _{n}$ is a sequence of the following type:\n\n\\begin{aligned} i_{1},\\ldots ,i_{n},-i_{1},\\ldots ,-i_{n}, \\end{aligned}\n\n$$\\begin{aligned} i_{1},\\ldots ,i_{n},-i_{1},\\ldots ,-i_{n}, \\end{aligned}$$\n\nwhere i_{1},\\ldots ,i_{n}$i_{1},\\ldots ,i_{n}$ form a facet. Thus, all zigzags are of length 2n. Since \\beta _{n}$\\beta _{n}$ has precisely 2^{n}$2^{n}$ facets, Lemma 3.7 shows that there are precisely 2^{n-2}(n-1)!$2^{n-2}(n-1)!$ zigzags.\n\nThe 0-shadows of zigzags considered in Examples 3.8 and 3.10 consist of all vertices of the complex. For the general case this fails.\n\nBy Lemma 3.2, every automorphism of \\Delta $\\Delta $ sends zigzags to zigzags. We say that \\Delta $\\Delta $ is z-transitive if for any two zigzags there is an automorphism of \\Delta $\\Delta $ transferring one of them to the other. The complexes \\alpha _{n}$\\alpha _{n}$, \\beta _{n}$\\beta _{n}$ are z-transitive. Also, they are z-simple.\n\nThe facets of \\Sigma (W,S)$\\Sigma (W,S)$ corresponding to w and v form a distance normal pair if and only if there is a reduced expression for w^{-1}v$w^{-1}v$ whose elements are mutually distinct (the existence of such an expression implies that all reduced expressions of w^{-1}v$w^{-1}v$ satisfy the same condition).\n\nLet X=X_{0},X_{1},\\ldots ,X_{m}=Y$X=X_{0},X_{1},\\ldots ,X_{m}=Y$ be a geodesic in \\Gamma _{n-1}(\\Delta )$\\Gamma _{n-1}(\\Delta )$. Then d(X,Y)=m$d(X,Y)=m$. We say that this geodesic is normal if any two X_{i},X_{j}$X_{i},X_{j}$ satisfying d(X_{i},X_{j})\\le n$d(X_{i},X_{j})\\le n$ form a distance normal pair (if m\\le n$m\\le n$, then this means that X and Y form a distance normal pair). If d(X,Y)>n$d(X,Y)>n$, then the fact that X and Y are connected by a normal geodesic does not guarantee that every geodesic connecting them is normal.\n\n### Example 4.5\n\nIt is not difficult to construct a thin chamber complex of rank 3 satisfying the following conditions:\n\n  * there are faces X, Y satisfying d(X,Y)=4$d(X,Y)=4$ and connected by a normal geodesic,\n\n  * there is a face X'$X'$ adjacent to X and intersecting Y precisely in a vertex,\n\n  * d(X',Y)=3$d(X',Y)=3$.\n\nSo, X'$X'$ and Y do not form a distance normal pair. Let X',X_{1},X_{2},Y$X',X_{1},X_{2},Y$ be a geodesic connecting X'$X'$ and Y. The the geodesic X,X',X_{1},X_{2},Y$X,X',X_{1},X_{2},Y$ is not normal.\n\nThe (n-1)$(n-1)$-shadows of zigzags in \\Delta $\\Delta $ are closed (not necessarily simple) paths in \\Gamma _{n-1}(\\Delta )$\\Gamma _{n-1}(\\Delta )$. We say that a path X_{1},\\ldots ,X_{m}$X_{1},\\ldots ,X_{m}$ is contained in a path Y_{1},\\ldots ,Y_{k}$Y_{1},\\ldots ,Y_{k}$ (or Y_{1},\\ldots ,Y_{k}$Y_{1},\\ldots ,Y_{k}$ contains X_{1},\\ldots ,X_{m}$X_{1},\\ldots ,X_{m}$) if there is a number j such that Y_{j+i}=X_{i}$Y_{j+i}=X_{i}$ for every i\\in \\{0,1,\\ldots ,m\\}$i\\in \\{0,1,\\ldots ,m\\}$. It is clear that every path contained in a normal geodesic is a normal geodesic.\n\n## About this article\n\n### Cite this article\n\nDeza, M., Pankov, M. Zigzag Structure of Thin Chamber Complexes. Discrete Comput Geom 59, 363–382 (2018). https://doi.org/10.1007/s00454-017-9954-z\n\n  * Received: 12 September 2015\n\n  * Revised: 20 October 2017\n\n  * Accepted: 07 November 2017\n\n  * Published: 01 December 2017\n\n  * Issue date: March 2018\n\n  * DOI (Digital Object Identifier): https://doi.org/10.1007/s00454-017-9954-z\n\n### Keywords\n\n  * Zigzag\n  * Thin chamber complex\n  * Coxeter complex\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nwilliams3351.dvi (https://www.cambridge.org/core/services/aop-cambridge-core/content/view/25E03AA9BEF84ED397EAC725AD194588/S0008414X00021283a.pdf/petrie-schemes.pdf)\nciteturn0search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4.6 years ago; Petrie Schemes\nGordon Williams ... This paper summarizes the author’s doctoral dissertation Petrie Schemes completed in 2002 at the Uni-\nCanad. J. Math. Vol. 57 (4), 2005 pp. 844–870\nPetrie Schemes\nGordon Williams\nAbstract. Petrie polygons, especially as they arise in the study of regular polytopes and Coxeter groups,\nhave been studied by geometers and group theorists since the early part of the twentieth century.\nAn open question is the determination of which polyhedra possess Petrie polygons that are simple\nclosed curves. The current work explores combinatorial structures in abstract polytopes, called Petrie\nschemes, that generalize the notion of a Petrie polygon. It is established that all of the regular convex\npolytopes and honeycombs in Euclidean spaces, as well as all of the Gr¨unbaum–Dress polyhedra, pos-\nsess Petrie schemes that are not self-intersecting and thus have Petrie polygons that are simple closed\ncurves. Partial results are obtained for several other classes of less symmetric polytopes.\n1\nIntroduction\nHistorically, polyhedra have been conceived of either as closed